From: Andre Eikmeyer <dev@deq.rocks>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: create and remove the AWDL interface

Firmware with AWDL support takes an interface of type AWDL from
interface_create. The host gives the MAC address, the firmware
interface index 2 and the AWDL BSSID 00:25:00:ff:94:73. The firmware
answers with an interface event like for any other interface.

The AWDL interface has no cfg80211 vif. Add brcmf_awdl_add() and
brcmf_awdl_del(), called with RTNL and the wiphy mutex held, which
create and remove it together with a network device "awdl%d":

- The interface events of the AWDL interface complete a wait in
  brcmf_awdl_add() and brcmf_awdl_del() and do not reach the cfg80211
  handler. A vif_event armed for a P2P or AP interface at the same time
  is not taken by the AWDL interface. When the firmware removes the AWDL
  interface on its own, the event worker removes it as before.
- Its other firmware events are not passed to the cfg80211 handlers.
- The network device gets its own net_device_ops before it is
  registered, without multicast list and MAC address change. The
  firmware filters AWDL traffic itself.
- brcmf_net_attach() and brcmf_net_detach() register and unregister a
  network device without wireless_dev with RTNL held.
- brcmf_awdl_add() refuses to run if firmware interface index 2 is in
  use or the firmware does not announce AWDL.
- The AWDL interface uses direct addressing like an AP, every peer
  gets its own flowrings. brcmf_awdl_del() deletes them before it
  stops AWDL and removes the interface.
- The AWDL interface is removed in the cfg80211 suspend callback while
  the firmware still takes commands. No new one is created once the
  device is being detached.

Signed-off-by: Andre Eikmeyer <dev@deq.rocks>
